## Known users

A user becomes known to Highstate after signing in or using local Designer.
Being known does not grant access to projects or backend resources.
Access comes from role bindings in the appropriate realm.

In **Data & Settings**, choose **Backend** and open **Backend Users** to see known human identities and their authentication source.
Switch to **Project**, select a project, and open **Users** to see the same directory, including people who have not opened that project yet.
This lets you assign someone's first project role without asking them to enter the project first.
The directory requires permission to list users in the selected realm.

## Managing a user's access

Select a user to view their identity details and direct role bindings.
Expand **Roles**, select one or more roles using the checkboxes, and choose **Add**.
Use a binding's row action to remove it.
If an assignment fails, bindings already added are kept. Fix the error and choose **Add** again to retry the remaining selection.
Backend bindings affect backend access; project bindings affect only the selected project.
Project access management requires an unlocked project.

Viewing bindings requires permission to read users, roles, and role bindings in that realm.
Changing a binding requires permission to update the target role.
If the role grants permissions beyond your own, you also need escalation permission.
Unavailable controls stay disabled and explain the missing permission when you hover over them.
User, binding, and permission changes appear automatically without a refresh button.

These controls manage direct user bindings, not access inherited from identity-provider groups.
Deployment-configured administrator grants are also managed separately and can be restored during initialization.
Removing a direct binding does not remove access granted through those other paths.
